In general, designers should attempt to: 
- Use detachable joints such as snap, screw or bayonet instead of welded, glued or soldered connections;
- Use standardized joints so that the product can be dismantled with a few universal tools, e.g., one type and size of screws;
- Position joints so that the product does not need to be turned or moved for dismantling;
- Indicate on the product how it should be opened non-destructively, e.g., where and how to apply leverage with a screwdriver to open snap connections;
- Place parts that are likely to wear out at the same time in close proximity so they can be easily replaced simultaneously. 
- Indicate on the product which parts must be cleaned or maintained in a specific way, e.g., colour-coded lubricating points;
- Create an intuitively disassembling process;